<h2>Thirty years in four stages</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>1996-1999: the auction</h3><p>DDA auctions the plot &ldquo;as is where is&rdquo; for Rs 60,05,000; possession follows in July 1997, but a boundary-wall encroachment is removed only in 1999 — and the plot is not in the sanctioned layout plan, so no building plan can be sanctioned.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>2004-2010: consumer forum to arbitrator</h3><p>The NCDRC finds deficiency in service; the Supreme Court refers the disputes to a sole arbitrator, whose 2010 award directs DDA to take steps to include the plot in the layout plan, bars ground rent until inclusion, and grants 9% interest plus costs.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>2020-2026: Section 34</h3><p>The Single Judge dismisses DDA\'s challenge on 17 February 2026; a review on pecuniary jurisdiction fails in September 2026 and is never separately challenged.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>2026: Section 37</h3><p>The Division Bench dismisses the appeal, affirming the award in full.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Every ground rejected</h2>
<p>On <strong>jurisdiction</strong>, DDA argued the matter fell below the commercial threshold; the Bench noted the combined claim and counterclaim exceeded Rs 2 crore and, decisively, that the review order of 3 September 2026 on the point stood unchallenged. On <strong>perversity</strong>, the arbitrator\'s finding that the plot was outside the layout plan was held not to rest on &ldquo;no evidence&rdquo; — the fact that a plot is identifiable and was auctioned does not prove it formed part of the approved layout plan. A later building-plan sanction, the Bench observed, &ldquo;does not, by itself, establish&rdquo; the plot\'s status at the 1996 auction.</p>
<p>The most instructive holding concerns <strong>additional evidence</strong>. DDA had sought to place 1984-era documents on record at the Section 34 stage. The Bench refused: the documents contradicted DDA\'s own earlier stand, and no explanation was offered for their non-production before the arbitrator. In its words, &ldquo;A party invoking Section 34 cannot be permitted to recast its factual case by introducing a materially different position.&rdquo; The directions in the award were also held not to breach the Delhi Development Act or amend the Master or Zonal Plans, and the ground-rent bar with 9% interest followed naturally from the layout-plan finding.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>The standard restated:</strong> a Section 37 court cannot sit &ldquo;in appeal over an arbitral award&rdquo; or undertake &ldquo;a fresh appraisal of the evidence.&rdquo; The appeal is a check on the Section 34 court\'s application of its own limited grounds — not a second merits review.</p>
</div>

<h2>Lessons for award challenges</h2>
<p>Three practice points emerge. First, the factual record closes with the arbitration: documents not produced before the tribunal will almost never be admitted at the Section 34 stage, least of all documents that contradict the challenger\'s earlier position. Parties must fight the facts where the facts are tried. Second, interlocutory and review orders matter — DDA\'s pecuniary-jurisdiction objection died not on its merits but because the order rejecting it was never separately challenged; objections must be kept procedurally alive at every rung. Third, public bodies enjoy no softer standard under Sections 34 and 37: the discipline of the award-challenge regime applies to DDA exactly as to a private party, and three decades of litigation over a single unbuildable plot is the cautionary tale. The operative order was brief: the appeal was dismissed, the Single Judge\'s judgment of 17 February 2026 affirmed, and pending applications closed.</p>
